{"step_index":2448,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T10:46:22Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-27T16:16:22+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-10-27T16:16:29+05:30\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\nTotal problems: 300\r\nFirst 10 problems:\r\n  #1 LC 1: Two Sum (Easy) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Hash Map Lookup\r\n  #2 LC 217: Contains Duplicate (Easy) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Hash Set\r\n  #3 LC 242: Valid Anagram (Easy) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Frequency Counter\r\n  #4 LC 49: Group Anagrams (Medium) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Categorization by Key\r\n  #5 LC 347: Top K Frequent Elements (Medium) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Bucket Sort / Frequency Map\r\n  #6 LC 238: Product of Array Except Self (Medium) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Prefix & Suffix Products\r\n  #7 LC 36: Valid Sudoku (Medium) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: 2D Hash Set Tracking\r\n  #8 LC 128: Longest Consecutive Sequence (Medium) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Hash Set Streak Building\r\n  #9 LC 169: Majority Element (Easy) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Boyer-Moore Voting\r\n  #10 LC 136: Single Number (Easy) - Topic: Arrays & Hashing - Pattern: Bitwise XOR\r\n\r\nTopics distribution:\r\n  Trees: 32\r\n  Arrays & Hashing: 30\r\n  Graphs: 24\r\n  1-D Dynamic Programming: 24\r\n  Linked List: 22\r\n  Two Pointers: 20\r\n  Stack: 20\r\n  Binary Search: 20\r\n  Sliding Window: 18\r\n  Backtracking: 18\r\n\n"}
{"step_index":2449,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T10:46:29Z","input_tokens":2692,"cache_read_tokens":166944,"output_tokens":4511,"thinking":"Baseline Two Sum solution utilizes brute force, checking all possible pairs of numbers within the input list. Its time complexity is quadratic, $O(n^2)$, because it iterates through all combinations, while its space complexity is constant, $O(1)$. This approach is straightforward but inefficient for large datasets.\n\n\nA hash map lookup provides a more efficient $O(n)$ solution for Two Sum by storing seen values and their indices. The optimized method iterates through the numbers, calculating the difference needed to reach the target and checking if that complement exists in the map. Crucially, the hash map insertion should happen *after* the existence check to prevent self-matches.\n\nThis optimized Two Sum pattern's efficiency can be generalized to other problems. For instance, problems like checking for duplicates, validating anagrams, or calculating products (excluding self) can also be reduced from a quadratic or logarithmic baseline to a linear time complexity using hash maps or prefix/postfix approaches.\n\n\nTwo Pointers offer $O(n)$ optimization for Container With Most Water by converging from both ends, focusing on the shorter height. Three Sum shifts to $O(n^2)$ via sorting and Two Pointers, skipping duplicates.\n\nSliding Window solves Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters in $O(n)$ with character index tracking, significantly faster than brute-force. Binary Search optimizes Search in Rotated Sorted Array to $O(\\log n)$ by halving the search space.\n\nBest Time to Buy and Sell Stock achieves $O(n)$ with a single pass, tracking the running minimum. Maximum Subarray is efficiently solved in $O(n)$ using Kadane's algorithm, tracking and resetting running sums.\n\n\nThe goal is to structure solutions, separating baseline and optimized approaches for educational purposes, including complexity analysis and common pitfalls. The designed data structure will hold problem-specific information alongside explanations, code, and verification status. This approach emphasizes understanding both inefficient and efficient solutions, along with their trade-offs.\n\n\nConsidering how to integrate detailed solutions into the existing system. The preferred location is within the existing `dsa_dashboard.js` file, alongside existing methods for teaching guides and test cases. This approach will return an object containing baseline and optimized solutions. The UI in the problem modal and workspace can be updated to render solution switcher tabs when multiple approaches exist, as well as render the solution information. The provided data structure encapsulates all relevant solution details.\n\n\nFocus shifted to implementing a verification script. The script's purpose is to rigorously validate both baseline and optimized solutions against a range of test cases. A Python execution environment will be employed for offline solution testing.\n\nThe verification process includes loading solutions, executing them against test data, and confirming the correctness of each outcome. This strategy emphasizes automated testing for comprehensive solution validation.\n\n\nThe goal is to develop dual solutions (baseline vs. optimized) for 30 topics.  This dual approach enhances learning by contrasting inefficient and efficient methodologies.  Each of the listed problems is well-suited for this educational approach.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"// scratch/test_phase6_solutions_engine.js\\n// Verify that baseline and optimized solutions execute cleanly in PythonExecutionEngine\\n\\nconst fs = require('fs');\\nconst vm = require('vm');\\nconst path = require('path');\\n\\nconst engineCode = fs.readFileSync('app_build/engine.js', 'utf8');\\nconst sb = { console, Math, Number, String, Array, Object, Set, Map, JSON, Date, window: {} };\\nvm.createContext(sb);\\nvm.runInContext(engineCode, sb);\\nconst engine = new sb.window.PythonExecutionEngine();\\n\\nfunction testSolution(name, code, callCode, expectedOutput) {\\n  const harness = `\\\\n${code}\\\\n__res__ = ${callCode}\\\\n`;\\n  const trace = engine.trace(harness, \\\"\\\");\\n  if (!trace.ok) {\\n    return { name, ok: false, error: trace.error };\\n  }\\n  const lastStep = trace.steps[trace.steps.length - 1];\\n  const v = (lastStep.variables || []).find(x => x.name === '__res__');\\n  const actual = v ? v.current : 'None';\\n  const normActual = String(actual).replace(/\\\\s+/g, '');\\n  const normExpected = String(expectedOutput).replace(/\\\\s+/g, '');\\n  const matches = (normActual === normExpected);\\n  return {\\n    name,\\n    ok: matches,\\n    actual,\\n    expected: expectedOutput,\\n    steps: trace.steps.length\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(\\\"Testing Sample Dual Approaches:\\\");\\n\\n// 1. Two Sum\\nconst twoSumBaseline = `\\ndef twoSum(nums, target):\\n    n = len(nums)\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\\n            if nums[i] + nums[j] == target:\\n                return [i, j]\\n    return []\\n`;\\nconst twoSumOptimized = `\\ndef twoSum(nums, target):\\n    prevMap = {}\\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\\n        diff = target - nums[i]\\n        if diff in prevMap:\\n            return [prevMap[diff], i]\\n        prevMap[nums[i]] = i\\n    return []\\n`;\\nconsole.log(\\\"1. Two Sum (Baseline):\\\", testSolution(\\\"TwoSum Baseline\\\", twoSumBaseline, \\\"twoSum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9)\\\", \\\"[0, 1]\\\"));\\nconsole.log(\\\"1. Two Sum (Optimized):\\\", testSolution(\\\"TwoSum Optimized\\\", twoSumOptimized, \\\"twoSum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9)\\\", \\\"[0, 1]\\\"));\\\n<truncated 2574 bytes>","Description":"\"Test sample solutions in engine\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\CVT\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\brain\\\\4b927fe1-f9a3-4b3f-9f8a-92760e157eef\\\\scratch\\\\test_phase6_solutions_engine.js\"","toolAction":"\"Writing test_phase6_solutions_engine.js\"","toolSummary":"\"Create solutions verification prototype\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
