{"step_index":2456,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:44Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-27T16:17:44+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-10-27T16:17:44+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/index.html`\nTotal Lines: 355\nTotal Bytes: 18345\nShowing lines 330 to 355\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n330:         <div id=\"advSymbolsText\" class=\"adv-body\"></div>\r\n331:       </div>\r\n332:       <div class=\"adv-box\">\r\n333:         <div class=\"adv-title\">Variables Before → After (With Addresses & Types)</div>\r\n334:         <div id=\"advFullVarsText\" class=\"adv-body\"></div>\r\n335:       </div>\r\n336:       <div class=\"adv-box\">\r\n337:         <div class=\"adv-title\">Execution Flowchart</div>\r\n338:         <div id=\"flowchartSvgWrap\" class=\"adv-flowchart\"></div>\r\n339:       </div>\r\n340:     </div>\r\n341:   </section>\r\n342:   </div>\r\n343:   <!-- End of visualizerViewContainer -->\r\n344: \r\n345:   <!-- Main Container for DSA Learning View -->\r\n346:   <main id=\"dsaLearningContainer\" class=\"dsa-learning-container hidden\" aria-label=\"DSA Learning Academy\"></main>\r\n347: \r\n348:   <script src=\"dsa_problems.js\"></script>\r\n349:   <script src=\"dsa_dashboard.js\"></script>\r\n350:   <script src=\"engine.js\"></script>\r\n351:   <script src=\"explainer.js\"></script>\r\n352:   <script src=\"app.js\"></script>\r\n353: </body>\r\n354: </html>\r\n355: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":2457,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T10:47:44Z","input_tokens":2751,"cache_read_tokens":175712,"output_tokens":9136,"thinking":"Adding the script tag `<script src=\"dsa_solutions_data.js\"></script>` between `dsa_problems.js` and `dsa_dashboard.js` within `app_build/index.html` will define `window.DSA_SOLUTIONS_REGISTRY`. This suggests a data dependency where solution data needs to be loaded before the dashboard attempts to use it.\n\n\nEach `window.DSA_SOLUTIONS_REGISTRY` entry should be keyed by `problemId`. The value associated with each key will be an object detailing solution information. This object must include the `problemId`, a boolean indicating `hasMultipleApproaches`, and an `approaches` array.\n\nEach element within the `approaches` array needs an `id`, `name`, and boolean `isBaseline`. Furthermore, each approach should have complexity metrics (`timeComplexity`, `spaceComplexity`), executable `code`, a textual `explanation` of the approach and `whyItWorks`, and a list of `commonMistakes`.\n\n\nThe problem requires a solution that finds two numbers in an array that add up to a target, returning their indices. The brute-force approach has O(n^2) time complexity, leading to time limit exceeded errors on larger arrays. The one-pass hash map approach appears optimal with O(n) time and space complexity by storing seen numbers and their indices for quick lookups.\n\n\nFocusing on the Two Sum problem, the prompt suggests a preference for returning earlier indices when duplicate numbers are present. Two-pointer convergence, given a sorted list, is being considered; however, it has a potential issue regarding preserving original indices.\n\n\nAnalyzing a series of common algorithmic problems and their baseline versus optimized approaches. Investigating stack-based list reversal as a baseline versus in-place pointer reversal for linked lists, and linear scan versus modified binary search for rotated sorted arrays. Comparing queue-based level order traversal against recursive DFS for binary tree depth calculation.\nExploring O(n^2) nested loops for buy/sell stock to O(n) running minimum, hash map frequency versus bitwise XOR for single number identification, BFS with visited sets against in-place DFS flood fill for island counting. Also, a BFS queue baseline versus recursive DFS post-order for tree inversion, all subarray sums O(n^2) versus Kadane's algorithm O(n) for max subarray, naive recursion for climbing stairs versus DP, and exhaustive search for coin change to dynamic programming. Considering a forward scan for daily temperatures against an optimized method.\n\n\nAnalyzing problems with both baseline and optimized approaches to build the solutions registry. Identified O(n^2) to O(n log n) improvements for interval merging and O(n) prefix sum hash map optimization for subarray sum problems. Considering the structure of the optimized reference solution to be generated, including complexity, code, explanation, and common mistakes. Planning the `scratch/build_solutions_registry.py` script to handle the data transformation.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"# scratch/build_solutions_registry.py\\nimport json\\nimport os\\n\\nwith open('app_build/dsa_problems.js', 'r', encoding='utf-8') as f:\\n    text = f.read()\\n\\nstart_idx = text.find('[')\\nend_idx = text.rfind(']')\\nproblems = json.loads(text[start_idx:end_idx + 1])\\nprint(f\\\"Loaded {len(problems)} problems from catalog\\\")\\n\\n# Curated canonical dual approaches (Baseline vs Optimized)\\nDUAL_APPROACHES = {\\n    # 1. Two Sum (LC 1)\\n    1: {\\n        \\\"hasMultipleApproaches\\\": True,\\n        \\\"approaches\\\": [\\n            {\\n                \\\"id\\\": \\\"baseline\\\",\\n                \\\"name\\\": \\\"Approach 1: Brute Force (Baseline)\\\",\\n                \\\"isBaseline\\\": True,\\n                \\\"timeComplexity\\\": \\\"O(n²)\\\",\\n                \\\"spaceComplexity\\\": \\\"O(1)\\\",\\n                \\\"code\\\": \\\"def twoSum(nums, target):\\\\n    n = len(nums)\\\\n    for i in range(n):\\\\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\\\\n            if nums[i] + nums[j] == target:\\\\n                return [i, j]\\\\n    return []\\\\n\\\\nnums = [2, 7, 11, 15]\\\\nprint(\\\\\\\"Indices:\\\\\\\", twoSum(nums, 9))\\\",\\n                \\\"explanation\\\": \\\"Iterate through every possible pair of elements using nested loops. For each pair (i, j) where j > i, check if their sum equals the target. Return their indices immediately upon finding a match.\\\",\\n                \\\"whyItWorks\\\": \\\"Exhaustively tests every unique unordered pair (i, j) with i < j. By the fundamental properties of addition, if any valid pair exists that sums to target, this search space is guaranteed to find it.\\\",\\n                \\\"commonMistakes\\\": [\\n                    \\\"Starting the inner loop at j = 0 or j = i instead of j = i + 1, which accidentally reuses the same element twice (e.g., matching nums[i] with itself).\\\",\\n                    \\\"Time limit exceeded (TLE) on large arrays: n² operations exceed the execution limit when n = 10,000.\\\",\\n                    \\\"Returning the values [nums[i], nums[j]] instead of the 0-based indices [i, j] requested by the problem.\\\"\\n                ],\\n             \n<truncated 23869 bytes>","Description":"\"Build solutions registry script\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\CVT\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\brain\\\\4b927fe1-f9a3-4b3f-9f8a-92760e157eef\\\\scratch\\\\build_solutions_registry.py\"","toolAction":"\"Writing build_solutions_registry.py\"","toolSummary":"\"Create solutions registry builder\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
